AUDIO: 'Pneumatics, are you go for test? &gt;&gt; This is pneumatics. We are go for test. Okay, we are going to commence test in five 4 3 2 1. We\'re going to make space radically more'

[00:17] [scene:hardware]
VISUAL: A view inside a large pipe or accelerator.
AUDIO: 'affordable and crack the solar system open like an egg. We\'re building a multi- injection gas accelerator to throw massive vehicles to orbital velocities and put them directly into orbit. Today we\'re doing for the first time'

[00:39] [scene:hardware]
VISUAL: A person standing next to a large pipe system with various sensors and equipment.
AUDIO: 'ever side injection onto a vehicle. &gt;&gt; The team was basically founded with this as part of the idea about how we would get up to space launch speeds and we\'re going to do it for the first time tonight. &gt;&gt; We\'ve been working really hard to make'

[00:53] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'sure that we understand all the physics that are involved here. So, we started off by doing some initial modeling, doing some CFD, initial 1D models, did some 2D modeling, and then we ended up doing some testing on a static rig. So, being able to get this deployed and'

[01:07] [scene:hardware]
VISUAL: A long view of the accelerator system in a hangar.
AUDIO: 'start validating our modeling. So, amazing. We\'ve been working so hard for this. So, it\'s awesome to finally have it deployed. I think the testing so far is going really well. I was responsible for setting up the gas systems. We\'re using'

[01:19] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'a different gas than we\'ve ever used before. So most of our shots in the past have been either nitrogen or helium if we want to go really fast. But for this test campaign, we are actually using carbon dioxide, which is interesting because it has a lower speed of sound.'

[01:32] [scene:hardware]
VISUAL: Close-up of a component being inserted into the pipe, labeled "TOHZAV".
AUDIO: 'And so having a gas with a slow speed of sound lets us do that testing at a lower velocity and get a clearer picture of the effect versus having to have a very big, very long system.'

[01:44] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: '&gt;&gt; We are at our target pressure and capacitor charge. We are going to commence the test in 5 4 3 2 1. &gt;&gt; One of the most satisfying things as an'

[02:01] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'engineer is when you can identify some physics that\'s out there that people have written about and then find a way to apply it and instantiate that physical phenomenon in the real world.'

[02:14] [scene:result]
VISUAL: A damaged projectile or component after a test.
AUDIO: 'So, people have written about side injection as a technique for bringing projectiles to really high speeds for decades. And nobody has ever actually taken one of those papers and turned it into real hardware. And now we have. We'

[02:26] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'modified our 6-in gun system to add side injectors to it and we fired a projectile supersonic speeds and targeted it and hit it with injections of gas. The timing requirements and the'

[02:38] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'geometries are exactly analogous to the system that will be required to send things to space launch velocities. So you scale this thing up by a factor of 20 in diameter, in length, in speed.'

[02:52] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'Everything stays the same in terms of timing requirements, but you\'re going to space. So it\'s really cool to finally get footage of gas injection on the side of the arrow wedge and see our'

[03:04] [scene:location]
VISUAL: Text on screen: "BARSTOW, CA Test campaign: October - December 2026" with a desert landscape.
AUDIO: 'projectile coming out. Our next test campaign, we\'re going to be taking our 6-in gun system out to Barstow, and we\'re going to be doing shots for speed. So, what that means in'

[03:17] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'this case is we\'re done using our slow speed gases like CO2 and nitrogen. We\'re going to be using full speed hydrogen. We\'re going to fire a lightweight projectile in excess of Mach 5 and'

[03:29] [scene:hardware]
VISUAL: The Longshot accelerator system assembled outdoors with balloons.
AUDIO: 'actually execute hypersonic test shot. We\'re also going to be doing test shots with a segment of our big iron accelerator where we\'re going to have several large pressure vessels and we\'ll be discharging those full of hydrogen'

[03:42] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'just to test out the dynamics of the barrel booster interfaces, things like that and de-risk an individual booster segment before we go and deploy 50 of those segments to turn it into a'

[03:54] [scene:facility]
VISUAL: A security camera view of people working in a facility.
AUDIO: 'hypersonic test bed. Because for Barstow we\'re going to use hydrogen which is a much more hazardous gas. We need to change our gas fill scheme from a manually operated system as we\'ve been using with CO2 and'

[04:08] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'nitrogen and all these inert gases to a remote system that we can actually operate from our command center so that an operator doesn\'t have to be next to the gas system. One of the primary figures of merit in'

[04:21] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'evaluating a light gas gun is the speed of sound of the gas. The faster that pressure wave can travel through the gas, the more effective the gas is at accelerating a projectile down the'

[04:34] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'barrel. We\'ve historically used nitrogen and it has a speed of sound of about 370 m/s. It\'s really cheap. It\'s quick and easy to use, but it\'s a very slow gas. Helium has a speed just north of 900 m/s. If you go to hydrogen, you go'

[04:47] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'another big chunk. Hydrogen\'s speed of sound is 1,270 m/s if it\'s cold. There is no gas that\'s lighter than hydrogen. So, you have to do other things if you want to have a higher speed of sound.'

[05:00] [scene:data]
VISUAL: A bar chart showing "SPEED OF SOUND IN THE WORKING GAS" for Nitrogen (370 m/s), Helium (900 m/s), Hydrogen (1270 m/s), and With Combustion (2100 m/s).
AUDIO: 'The most obvious one is to increase the temperature of the gas. One of the most efficient ways to get heat into a gas is to combust it. If we add a small amount of oxygen to the hydrogen and then combust it, we get a mixture of gases'

[05:13] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'that altogether has a much higher speed of sound. Something like 2100 m/s instead of just 1,270 m/s. So we can almost double again the speed of sound of the fluid, which basically can uh'

[05:26] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'again just about double the performance of the system from a speed perspective. It has the auxiliary bonus that combustion also increases the pressure in the gas, which means that rather than'

[05:38] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'statically pressurizing our gas to say 1,000 PSI, we only have to get the gas up to about 200 PSI and then the combustion takes care of the rest of it. What that means is that we can use 1/5 as much gas as we need to get twice the'

[05:51] [scene:hardware]
VISUAL: A close-up of a test rig with multiple connections and sensors.
AUDIO: 'performance. So, it\'s about a 10x improvement to do combustion versus cold hydrogen. We\'ve built a test rig to basically test out a bunch of different ignition timing'

[06:03] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'techniques and calibration techniques to determine the right gas mixture, the right ignition timing, the right ignition mechanisms, and a whole bunch of different performance characteristics to track the movement of the pressure'

[06:16] [scene:facility]
VISUAL: An aerial view of the Longshot facility near a port.
AUDIO: 'wave and the flame front down a linear tube. That set of testing will be really important when we want to scale up combustion to larger sizes and figure out the best ways to use combustion to accelerate a projectile down the barrel.'

[06:29] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'The team has tripled in size since I joined the company a year ago. We\'ve made amazing progress with the technical'

[06:42] [scene:hardware]
VISUAL: People standing next to a large curved metal structure.
AUDIO: 'development that\'s being led by NATO and Alex and Marius and the team. We\'re in a brand new beautiful facility in a hanger that you can see behind me. We\'re building cool hypersonic aircraft and we\'ve got our first gigantic 3 m'

[06:54] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'diameter segment for our orbital launch system. So, I mean, we have made a ton of progress in the last year. &gt;&gt; I am excited for the team. This side injection testing is a crucial lynch pin into making what this big vision for'

[07:08] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'Longot Space Technologies is horizontal space launch and really changing the paradigm of space launch. One of the things that humanity always does is whenever it finds a better way to move things in transportation, the world kind'

[07:23] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'of moves in that direction. I mean, it happened with steamships, it happens with railroads, it happens, it happened with airplanes, and the same thing will happen here. Rockets have been very effective. But having a way to launch'

[07:35] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'bulk cargo every single day to space affordably is going to change everything. Come back and check in with us cuz you\'re going to love the progress we\'re making as we go hypersonic fast and'

[07:47] [scene:talk]
